<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Gelnhausen</h1><p class="st-meta">1170 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Gelnhausen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Town on the Kinzig founded by Frederick Barbarossa, whose imperial palace hosted the 1180 diet that stripped Henry the Lion of his duchies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Emperor Frederick Barbarossa</a> founded Gelnhausen in 1170 as a market town, granting it privileges and building an imperial palace on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinzig-main">Kinzig</a> river. It lay on the trade road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. In 1180 the palace hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diet</a> that issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelnhausen-charter">Gelnhausen Charter</a>, dividing the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As an imperial city Gelnhausen was governed by a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> under the emperor's protection, prospering from trade along the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and the east. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marienkirche-gelnhausen">Marienkirche</a>, with an unusual eastern tower group, and the ruined palace are among the finest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> buildings. The town was too small to defend its autonomy for long once Hohenstaufen power collapsed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1349 King Charles IV pledged the city to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-schwarzburg">Counts of Schwarzburg</a>, and the pledge later passed to the Counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hanau-munzenberg">Hanau</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a>, which ruled it jointly. It kept a nominal imperial status until 1803, when it went to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hesse">Hesse-Kassel</a>. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1170 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.99%;width:12.60%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1170 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gelnhausen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gelnhausen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-gelnhausen" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Gelnhausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4890 432 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Gelnhausen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5210 688L5229 694M5210 688L5215 656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5210 688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5210 688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gelnhausen. <a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-gelnhausen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
